{"causes":["The Greek city-state of Athens nurtured a vibrant poetic tradition that spread throughout the Mediterranean world.","Influenced by ancient Mesopotamian epics like the Epic of Gilgamesh, Homer's Odyssey became a foundational text for Western literature.","Poetry's role in African oral traditions allowed for the transmission of cultural knowledge and myths across generations."],"effects":["Shaped Western literature with works like the Iliad and the Divine Comedy.","Inspired art movements like Surrealism and Symbolism.","Provided a platform for marginalized voices, including women and minorities, to express themselves."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Ancient Athens","sourceName":"Ancient Athens","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Poetry","targetName":"Poetry","context":"The city-state's patronage of the arts led to the development of a distinctive poetic style."},{"sourceSlug":"Homer's Odyssey","sourceName":"Homer's Odyssey","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"Western Literature","targetName":"Western Literature","context":"This epic poem became a foundational text for Western literature, shaping the genre of epic poetry."},{"sourceSlug":"African Oral Traditions","sourceName":"African Oral Traditions","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Cultural Knowledge","targetName":"Cultural Knowledge","context":"Poetry played a crucial role in the transmission of cultural myths and legends across generations."}],"places":[{"name":"Ancient Athens","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Beijing, China","role":"Major battle site"},{"name":"Cairo, Egypt","role":"Major literary center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: poetry","type":"Reference article","slug":"poetry"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q482","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry"],"tags":["academic discipline","Technological Innovations","scientific-paradigm"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"poetry is remembered as a landmark idea of the Classical era, referenced in 241 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.","summary":"Poetry is one of the oldest and most universal forms of human artistic expression — the use of language in structured, rhythmically organised, or aesthetically heightened ways to evoke emotion, communicate experience, encode cultural memory, and explore the limits of meaning. Poetry predates writing: the earliest human cultures used rhythmic, formulaic, and mnemonic oral verse for religious ritual, mythology, history, and wisdom literature — from the Vedic hymns of ancient India (c. 1500 BCE in written form, but far older in oral tradition) to the Homeric epics of ancient Greece (c. 800 BCE written form) to the Psalms of ancient Israel. The impulse to shape language into something beyond prose — to select, compress, intensify, and pattern — appears to be universal across human cultures and epochs.

The history of world poetry encompasses an extraordinary diversity of forms, traditions, and functions: the epic poetry of Homer's Iliad and Odyssey, Virgil's Aeneid, and the Sanskrit Mahabharata and Ramayana; the lyric poetry of Sappho, Horace, Li Bai, Hafez, and Shakespeare's sonnets; the devotional poetry of the Psalms, Rumi's Masnavi, and St. John of the Cross; the satirical poetry of Juvenal and Dryden; the Romantic poetry of Keats, Shelley, and Byron; the modernist revolution of Eliot's 'The Waste Land' (1922), which fragmented traditional form to reflect the dislocations of modernity; and the contemporary global proliferation of poetic forms that crosses linguistic, cultural, and digital boundaries. Each tradition has developed its own formal conventions — the Chinese regulated verse (lüshi), the Japanese haiku, the Persian ghazal, the Italian sonnet, the English blank verse — that represent millennia of accumulated aesthetic wisdom about how language can be shaped to achieve particular effects.

Poetry's social functions have been as diverse as its forms: as the primary vehicle of cultural memory before writing; as liturgical language in religious ritual; as political propaganda (in Homer's Iliad to Soviet-era odes); as a vehicle of romantic love, grief, and celebration; as a medium of philosophical and scientific instruction (Lucretius's 'De Rerum Natura', Hesiod's 'Works and Days'); and as the most compressed and intense form of aesthetic experience available in language.","subjects":["Literature","Art Forms","Classical Era","Aesthetics","Cultural History","Oral Tradition","Language","World Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT","WORLD_SYSTEMS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Poetry has had a profound impact on Western literature and culture, shaping the genre of epic poetry and providing a platform for marginalized voices.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
